; expect 42
; The two halves of a trait declaration that parsed and were dropped:
; a method's DEFAULT BODY and a SUPERTRAIT.
;
; A default meant an implementation had to repeat every method the
; trait declared, however mechanical - `ne` is `(if (eq x y) false
; true)` for every type there will ever be, and every `impl` had to
; say so. A supertrait meant nothing at all: `(trait (Ord a) (Eq a)
; ...)` declared a requirement nothing checked, so an `impl (Ord T)`
; with no `impl (Eq T)` behind it compiled until a call to `eq` from
; inside `Ord`'s own default went looking.
;
;   (ne 1 2)      the DEFAULT, generated for Int because `Eq Int`
;                 does not define it. Its own `(eq x y)` dispatches,
;                 which is the whole point and the part that needs the
;                 generated definition to carry the CONCRETE parameter
;                 types - eta-expanding it instead leaves `x` a type
;                 variable and the call reaches `opt` as an undefined
;                 `@eq`.
;   (lte 2 2)     a default that uses a method of the SUPERTRAIT.
;   (lt 1 2)      a written method, beside the generated ones.
;   (eq 3 3)      the supertrait's own method, still dispatching.
(trait (Eq a) where (
  eq :: (-> a a Bool)
  ne :: (-> a a Bool) = (lambda (x y)
    (if (eq x y)
      false
      true
    )
  )
))

(trait (Ord a) (Eq a) where (
  lt :: (-> a a Bool)
  lte :: (-> a a Bool) = (lambda (x y)
    (if (lt x y)
      true
      (eq x y)
    )
  )
))

(impl (Eq Int) where (
  (eq
    (lambda (x y) (== x y))
  )
))

(impl (Ord Int) where (
  (lt
    (lambda (x y) (< x y))
  )
))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (if (&&
    (&&
      (ne 1 2)      (if (ne 1 1)
        false
        true
      )
    )    (&& (&& (lte 2 2) (lte 1 2)) (&& (lt 1 2) (eq 3 3)))
  )
    42
    1
  )
)
